out.push(p('⚠ **The second heading decides this paper and it is where the marks are lost.** ⚠ **Candidates write a second heading that is the first heading with a qualification** — ***progress has been slower in speaking* — **which is not a negative finding and earns neither of the two marks.** **The negative heading must name something that has NOT changed and give it a figure.**'));
out.push(p('⚠ **And do not accept *that staff practise speaking more* as a recommendation.** ⚠ **It is 13.3\'s clause obliging nobody and 24.3\'s *make time for*, arriving in the last examination task of the level** — ⚠ **and a candidate who writes it has demonstrated, in the final paper, the one fault the whole level was organised to remove.**'));
out.push(stage('6P', 'Check together'));
out.push(p('**What to fail.** ⚠ **An adjective where a document was asked for.** ⚠ **A second heading that is positive with a qualification.** ⚠ **A negative finding with no figure.** ⚠ **A recommendation addressed to habits.** ⚠ **And a word count outside 120 to 140.**'));
out.push(stage('7', 'Write freely'));
out.push(p('⚠ **Mark the three acts and what the credential does not evidence.** ⚠ **The strongest texts are usually about a credential that is not a language one** — **a forklift certificate, a driving licence, a food-handling card** — **because nobody is tempted to describe a person with those, and the structure comes out clean.** ⚠ **Then ask the writer to apply the same three columns to their own B2 certificate, which is harder and is the point.**'));

out.push(pb());
out.push(stage('✓', 'Marking the unit check', 15));
out.push(table(['**Q**', '1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8'], [['Answer', 'b', 'a', 'c', 'b', 'a', 'b', 'a', 'c']], [1.2, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.6]));
out.push(box('Marking the unit check', [
  '**A  Language — 8 marks**, one each. ⚠ **Question 8 is the only one that tests the unit rather than a structure:** ***yet*, because the sentence is about a document written before anything in it is true.**',
  '**B  Listening — 6 marks**, one each. ⚠ **the September note, the one correcting her own earlier note, which she nearly left out (1) · that it was the only document he had been sent that year by anybody that corrected its own author, and that he asked for it first for that reason (1) · that *fluent* would have cost her the post, and that he wanted to write it because it would have made him feel generous (1) · a copy of the reference, which nobody had offered her, because she had never seen what she could do written down as things she had done (1) · read one document aloud to Lina on the first Monday** — ⚠ **four of four, and on the second she had not prepared anything and Lina asked anyway (1) · she does not know, and that is the point of having written this year\'s figure down (1).**',
  '**C  Reading — 5 marks:** ⚠ **a performance on 11 December against published criteria, not a description of a person (1)** · **three acts: read a long document under time pressure and found what it required; wrote to a word limit, twice; did both on a day she did not choose (2)** · ⚠ **the note of 19 September correcting her own earlier note, included because it is the first document she would ask for if she were the reader (2).**',
  '**D  Writing — 6 marks:** **the credential named (1)** · **what it evidences, as acts rather than qualities (2)** · ⚠ **what it does not evidence (2)** · **and a document of the writer\'s own, with a date (1).**',
  '**Total 25.** ⚠ **The profile to expect: A high, B split, C high, D split.** ⚠ **B loses the third gap, which asks what Mr Sayegh now says about his own motive** — **candidates record that he would not write the sentence again and omit** *because it would have made me feel generous*, **which is the admission the gap is for.** ⚠ **And D loses the *as acts* marks in about half of papers**, **where the writer lists qualities** — **the same fault as 24.2\'s *improved*, in the last written task of the level and after two cycles of warning.**',
], C.lteal, C.navy));
out.push(note("Teacher's note, and it is the last of the level. Six faults to watch, one warning, and one thing to do at the end. The warning first: this unit is not a reflection and must not be taught as one. Every cycle produces a document with a figure in it — a count, a date, a number of sentences, a gap expressed as one in nine — and a class that leaves having had feelings has done nothing. The instrument that keeps it honest is the claim-or-hope grading from 24.1, so teach that in the first ten minutes and use it in every cycle afterwards. First fault: reading an old page sympathetically. Run the three acts as a procedure and time the first reading; ninety seconds for a page. Expect learners to report that the second reading was already a defence, and expect the longest sentence on every page to be the one that cannot be graded. Insist that old sentences are quoted exactly and not improved, because tidying them destroys the evidence and the untidiness is usually the finding. Second fault: the word improved. It is not banned for vagueness. It is banned because nobody can disagree with it, which means nobody can agree with it either. Replace it with the four parts — the act, the reader, the date, the outcome — and insist on the fifth, which is the part the writer got wrong. Learners resist that hardest in the one document where it matters most, and the answer is arithmetic: four points and three successes is a document, four points and four successes is an advertisement, and the reader of an advertisement starts wondering what the fifth point was. Third fault, and this is the one to protect from the class: kindness in 24.3. Somebody will tell the first learner who says three times in two years that it is understandable, and the exercise ends there. Say so in advance. The figure is the finding. Insist on disqualifying instances, run the reason-or-excuse test as arithmetic rather than as a judgement — how many of your other undertakings were kept in the same period, and what was different about their wording — and end on a rewrite, because the fault is in a sentence and a sentence can be fixed by Friday. Fourth fault: the single count. A count of your own undertakings made by you is the least reliable count available and the only one anybody ever does. Enforce the rule that the second counter does not see the learner's marked list, expect the disagreement to turn on one word, usually a noun, and record the gap as a figure even though it is useless this year. And hold the hard line on back-dating: a late note dated today and headed as late is a record, the same note dated to the decision is a false document, and it converts an omission nobody would have pursued into a question about the whole file that cannot be answered. Fifth fault: the responsibility written in May. The December-not-May argument is about documents and not about character — a responsibility written after it starts is written to fit what has happened. Insist on the not-answerable-for half, numbered in the same sequence, and on the ownership column with a name and a date in it. Expect two of three learners to find a second claimant, expect neither party to have mentioned it, and do not let the class call that carelessness: it is the ordinary state of a handover, and the document exists because both people are reasonable. Sixth fault: the letter with no reader. A sealed letter has none; it depends on somebody remembering to bring the drawer. The act must be asks me for it and stays, which cannot be thanked and postponed, and the agreement must be in the reader's handwriting. About a third of a class will have a name and about a tenth will have an agreement, so send the rest out to ask before the envelopes are sealed, not afterwards. On 24.7, teach the certificate in both directions and in that order: what it does evidence before what it does not, or the class will declare it worthless within a minute. And make sure they see who decides the wording. The reference is about a person who is in the room, which happens nowhere else in two books, and a reference in which that person has had no voice is a document written for the referee's comfort. Finally, the thing to do at the end. The fourteen envelopes go in a drawer and the whole level has been an argument that a document with no arranged reader is a hope. So before the class disperses, make every learner name their reader out loud, say the date, and say whether that person has agreed. Write the fourteen names and dates on one sheet, date it, and put it where the envelopes are. It takes six minutes and it is the only part of this unit that cannot be done later."));

if (require.main === module) L.build(out, process.argv[2], 'Proactive Business English B2 · Book 2 · Syria Edition · Unit 24 · Answer Key');
module.exports = out;
